  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002738",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches in concept for the target (alcohol dependence): PGS002738 and PGS002739 explicitly map to trait_efo=\"alcohol dependence\" with phenotyping_reported=\"Alcohol use disorder\", while PGS000201/PGS000202 are labeled \"Problematic alcohol use\" but their phenotyping_reported is also AUD DSM-5 criteria count (log-transformed), which is closely aligned to alcohol dependence/AUD.\n\nPGS002738 is the best-supported single choice from the visible evidence because it has the most directly aligned trait mapping (trait_efo=\"alcohol dependence\"), a straightforward AUD endpoint, and the strongest reported discriminative signal among the set via an OR (top 10% vs rest) of 1.96 (1.54–2.51) in European evaluation (n=7,900). In contrast, PGS000201/PGS000202 report only small incremental ΔR² values (0.01192 and 0.00456) and do not provide OR/HR-style discrimination; PGS002739 has a weaker OR (1.76) and is evaluated only in African American/Afro-Caribbean samples, making it less generally supported absent a specified deployment ancestry.\n\nMain limitation: none of the candidates report PRS-only AUC or PRS-only R², and covariates are \"Unknown\" for PGS002738/PGS002739, reducing comparability and preventing a high-confidence selection based purely on PRS-comparable metrics."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 19 candidates are direct matches to angina pectoris (trait_efo aligned), spanning UKB-based snpnet framework scores (PGS001260/61/62), a UKB multi-PRS model (PGS000703), two 2024 UKB scores with only ORs and unknown covariates (PGS004457/PGS004527), and multiple MVP-based method-comparison scores (PGS005047–PGS005059) reporting full-model C-index and ORs.

PGS001262 is preferred because it has strong endpoint fidelity via an explicit doctor-diagnosed angina phenotype (phenotyping_reported: "Vascular/heart problems diagnosed by doctor Angina"), reports PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC 0.58683; PGS R2 0.01304) plus incremental AUROC (0.0098), and uses only basic covariates (age, sex, array type, genotype PCs). Its PGS-only R2 is also plausibly below the provided EUR heritability ceiling (h2_obs≈0.009–0.014; liability h2≈0.134), supporting metric sanity.

Main limitation: PGS001262 comes from a pan-trait UKB framework publication ("across 813 traits"), which domain guidance flags as often less reliable in external validation; additionally, several MVP-based candidates show higher full-model C-index (~0.76) and moderate-to-strong ORs (~1.43–1.49), but those metrics are not clearly PRS-only and are therefore not directly comparable. This leaves residual uncertainty, so the direct-match choice is supported but not high-confidence."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005252",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for the target trait (aortic stenosis) are present (PGS005252, PGS005254, PGS005255, PGS005256). Among these, PGS005252 has the strongest endpoint alignment (trait_reported/trait_efo both aortic stenosis; phenotyping_reported: incident aortic stenosis cases) and the richest comparable performance evidence in the provided metadata, including a reported validation discrimination metric (C-index 0.87) plus an effect size (HR 1.92). Competing direct-match models (PGS005254/PGS005255/PGS005256) also use incident aortic stenosis phenotyping but provide only HRs (1.64 and 1.53) and no AUC/C-index in the visible fields, making them harder to prefer on performance. Main limitation: PGS005252’s reported C-index is a full-model metric with substantial clinical covariate adjustment (multiple comorbidities/risk factors), and no PRS-only AUC/R² is shown for any candidate here, so the incremental PRS contribution cannot be cleanly compared; this keeps confidence at Moderate despite selecting the best-supported direct-match option from the available evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004944",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 15 candidates are direct matches to glaucoma or its dominant clinical subtype primary open-angle glaucoma (POAG), with generally acceptable endpoint alignment (most are POAG or glaucoma). Among these, PGS004944 is best supported by a combination of (i) endpoint fidelity (POAG; explicitly self-reported but still the target disease), (ii) large external evaluation sample size (n=407,667), and (iii) reported effect size (OR=1.74) plus a reported AUROC (0.748) from the selected performance record. However, the evidence remains sub-optimal because the AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (no explicit PGS-only AUROC/R2 is provided) and covariates include age/age^2/sex/ancestry, making cross-model comparability limited. Competing disease-focused candidates (e.g., PGS001792 PRS-CS-auto with AUROC 0.777 and very large evaluation n=347,396; PGS000137 with full-model AUROC 0.8 but includes family history; and UKB framework/portability scores with partial-r or incremental AUROC) prevent a clearly unambiguous winner based solely on PRS-comparable metrics visible here."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004160","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Multiple direct-match gout PRS candidates are present with broadly consistent endpoint alignment (trait_efo=gout; phenotyping_reported typically “Gout”/diagnosis/incident gout). However, the evidence does not identify a single clearly dominant model because many candidates report only full-model AUROC/C-index (often with covariates=0) or non-comparable effect sizes, and PRS-only metrics are sparse/inconsistent across the set.

PGS004160 is the best-supported choice from the visible evidence because it is a direct gout endpoint (phenotyping_reported: “Gout”), uses an explicit multi-score aggregation approach (UKBB-EUR.MultiPRS.CV; structurally favored by the provided domain knowledge), and shows the strongest full-model discrimination among the coherent “Evaluation of polygenic scoring methods in five biobanks…” gout cluster (full_model_auc=0.6936; R²=0.0893; OR=2.05) with a reasonably large validation sample (n=90,274) and no added covariates (covariates=0), reducing concern that performance is covariate-driven.

Main limitation: the key PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC / PGS-only R²) are not reported for PGS004160 (nor for many competitors), so selection relies on full-model metrics and structural considerations; additionally, other direct-match models (e.g., PGS001248/PGS001249 with explicit PGS-only AUROC/R², and PGS004265–PGS004269 with similar AUROC ~0.798) remain plausible alternatives, preventing a high-confidence single winner based solely on the provided metadata."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002763","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches at the disease-concept level (hip osteoarthritis / coxarthrosis / hip OA-related arthroplasty endpoints). The strongest endpoint-fidelity candidates for generic hip osteoarthritis are those with phenotyping_reported explicitly as hip osteoarthritis (PGS002763) or incident hip osteoarthritis (PGS004882), whereas total hip replacement/arthroplasty (PGS002728, PGS002750) is a treatment/proxy endpoint and time-to-event coxarthrosis (PGS000967) comes from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework paper, which domain guidance flags as a generalization risk despite having explicit PGS-only metrics.

PGS002763 is preferred because it has direct hip osteoarthritis phenotyping_reported (not a treatment proxy), uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRS-CS; 1,052,273 variants), and provides a PRS-comparable effect size (OR=1.38 per SD, 95% CI 1.31–1.46) with only basic covariates (age, sex, PCs, technical covariates). Competing direct-endpoint models either lack comparable PRS-only metrics and have weaker/less interpretable evidence (PGS004478/PGS004548: unknown covariates and only modest ORs), or are packaged as incidence/cumulative-incidence framework outputs (PGS004882) without PRS-only discrimination metrics.

Main limitation: across the pool, PRS-only AUC/R² is largely missing for the disease-focused candidates; the only explicit PRS-only metrics appear in the pan-trait framework model (PGS000967), which creates unresolved tension between metric availability and framework-penalized study archetype. This keeps confidence below high."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005265","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept (hyperthyroidism) via either explicit hyperthyroidism/thyrotoxicosis endpoints (PGS001042/PGS001043/PGS001815/PGS002023) or Graves’ disease (PGS005264/PGS005265/PGS005266), which domain_knowledge explicitly treats as a clinically dominant form of hyperthyroidism and therefore a strong direct match when competing alternatives are older UKB framework/portability scores.

PGS005265 is the best-supported choice from the visible evidence because it comes from a disease-focused, multi-biobank, multi-ancestry study context (many contributing cohorts; MAE evaluation) and uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRSCS) with very large variant count (~1.09M). It also reports a strong per-SD effect size (OR≈1.63), which domain_knowledge treats as meaningful evidence of PRS discriminative power, and its reported full-model AUROC (~0.665) is in the same general range as other candidates’ PRS-only AUROC (~0.63) without being an extreme outlier.

Main limitation: the Graves’ disease models report only full-model AUROC (no PGS-only AUROC/R²) and have unknown covariates, making performance comparability to the UKB snpnet models (which do report PGS-only AUROC/R²) uncertain. Additionally, there are multiple very similar Graves’ disease siblings (PGS005265 vs PGS005266) with near-identical metadata and AUROC, so the preference for PGS005265 rests mainly on the stronger reported OR rather than clearly superior PRS-only metrics."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000054","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All five candidates are direct matches at the concept level (trait_efo = late-onset Alzheimer's disease). Endpoint fidelity is strongest for models whose phenotyping_reported is the disease itself: PGS000334 and PGS004918 report "Late-onset Alzheimer’s disease" directly; PGS000053/PGS000054 report "Familial late-onset Alzheimer's disease (LOAD)" (still aligned to late-onset AD per provided domain guidance); PGS002289 is weaker on endpoint fidelity because its phenotyping_reported is a cognitive-test interaction phenotype rather than AD diagnosis.

Among the endpoint-faithful candidates, only PGS000053 and PGS000054 provide PRS-comparable effect-size evidence (OR per SD). PGS000054 shows a stronger reported association (OR=1.73, 95% CI 1.57–1.93) than PGS000053 (OR=1.29, 95% CI 1.21–1.37), which is supportive of better PRS discrimination per the domain guidance. The other candidates either report only a full-model AUROC (PGS004918; not a valid substitute for PRS-only performance) or an R² value (PGS000334) that cannot be cleanly interpreted as PRS-only and is flagged by the domain guidance as potentially suspect when high for simple C+T with unknown covariates.

This remains sub-optimal because key comparability fields are missing or limiting across the pool: no candidate reports a clear PGS-only AUROC/R²; covariates are unknown for several models; and the selected model (PGS000054) is evaluated in AMR/Hispanic/Latin American only, which may reduce generalizability if the intended deployment ancestry differs (not specified in context)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches at the disease-concept level for the target “nodular goiter”: two are for “Nontoxic multinodular goiter” (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three are for “Benign nodular goiter” (PGS005261, PGS005262, PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273, PGS005274) are case-control contrasts “Thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter” and are less endpoint-faithful to generic nodular goiter risk because the outcome is a differential-diagnosis contrast rather than nodular goiter status.

Among the most endpoint-aligned set (the three “Benign nodular goiter” models), PGS005262 is best supported by the visible evidence because it has the strongest reported discrimination within that sibling group (full-model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876) and the largest per-SD effect size (OR 1.408 vs 1.048 and 1.362), and it uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRSCS) with a very large variant set (~1.09M), which is structurally favorable compared with the P+T alternative.

The main limitation is that the reported AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (no explicit PGS-only AUC/R2 is provided) and covariates are listed as “Unknown,” reducing comparability and making it hard to judge standalone PRS performance; additionally, the UKB portability-framework multinodular-goiter models provide only weak partial-correlation metrics. These gaps prevent a high-confidence, clearly high-quality direct-match designation."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001791","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates are direct/near-direct matches for the target concept (pulmonary fibrosis), but two are specifically for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (PGS001791, PGS004695) rather than the broader pulmonary fibrosis label (PGS001030), creating some endpoint-scope ambiguity for a generic target.

PGS001791 is the best-supported choice from the visible evidence because it uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRS-CS-auto) with very large variant count (910,439) and comes from a large multi-biobank framework with European evaluation; it also reports a PRS-comparable metric (Nagelkerke R2 with covariates regressed out = 0.005929), which is interpretable as PRS signal and is below the provided heritability ceiling sanity-check. In contrast, PGS001030 is from the known pan-trait sparse UKB framework (“across 813 traits”) with only 51 variants; its very high reported AUROC (0.8015) is a full-model metric and the incremental/PGS-only metrics are modest, consistent with covariate-driven discrimination. PGS004695 is endpoint-faithful for incident idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and reports a strong HR (high vs low PRS quintile), but it lacks PRS-only AUC/R2 and uses a small GWAS-hits SNP set (23), making its standalone PRS comparability and general PRS strength harder to judge from the provided metadata.

Main limitation: the selected model targets idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis rather than the broader pulmonary fibrosis umbrella, and the available performance evidence across candidates is not fully comparable (PRS-only metrics missing for some; full-model AUROC not a substitute), so the recommendation cannot be rated high-confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000922","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All four candidates are direct matches at the disease concept level, but endpoint fidelity differs: PGS000922 and PGS000923 map to sarcoidosis (trait_efo=sarcoidosis) with phenotyping_reported as sarcoidosis/TTE sarcoidosis, whereas PGS001872 and PGS002084 have trait_efo=skin sarcoidosis (a narrower subtype), making them less clearly aligned to the generic target trait.

Between the two best-aligned candidates (PGS000922 vs PGS000923), both come from the same pan-trait UK Biobank sparse PRS framework paper (a known weaker study archetype for external generalization per the provided domain knowledge), use similar basic covariates, and have similar validation context (selected_validation_ancestry=European; validation_sample_size n=67,425). PGS000923 shows slightly higher reported full-model AUROC/R² than PGS000922, but it is explicitly time-to-event (phenotyping_reported=TTE sarcoidosis), which is a noted endpoint warning signal for a generic disease-risk target when not clearly outweighed by other evidence. Given the near-tie in metrics and shared framework limitations, the more straightforward disease-status endpoint (PGS000922) is preferred.

Main limitation: the top candidates are framework-derived sparse UKB scores and the alternative (skin sarcoidosis) models lack PRS-comparable AUROC/R², so the evidence does not support a clearly high-quality, externally robust choice."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005220",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present for sleep apnea, including multiple generic “Sleep apnea” models (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several “Obstructive sleep apnea” models (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Domain knowledge states obstructive sleep apnea is the dominant clinical form of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled models are acceptable direct matches when better supported.\n\nAmong OSA candidates, PGS005220 is best supported by the visible evidence: it targets obstructive sleep apnea, uses a multi-score aggregation approach (“weighted PRSsummation PRS-CSs”), reports strong effect size (OR=1.53 per SD, 95% CI 1.39–1.68) and a relatively high reported full-model AUROC (0.79), and is developed/validated in a multi-cohort context (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP) with multi-ancestry evaluation noted and a moderate validation sample size (n=21,975). In contrast, the ExPRSweb “Sleep apnea” framework cluster shows near-null discrimination (full-model AUROC ~0.49–0.53) and weak effect sizes, and PGS003479 has unclear phenotype coherence (phenotyping_reported listed as “DBP”) with no AUROC.\n\nMain limitation: none of the candidates provide explicit PRS-only (covariate-free) AUROC/R2; the high AUROC values for PGS005220/PGS005219 are full-model and include BMI (a strong risk factor), so the PRS-specific contribution cannot be isolated from the provided metadata. This prevents a high-confidence, high-quality designation despite the stronger overall evidence package for PGS005220."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004253",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 5 candidates are direct matches to ulcerative colitis by trait_efo, and most also by trait_reported/phenotyping_reported. Two candidates (PGS001306, PGS001307) come from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework paper (“across 813 traits”), which domain guidance flags as a systematic external-generalization weakness despite having explicit PRS-only metrics. Two others (PGS001855, PGS002066) come from a UKB portability framework paper and have limited/less comparable performance reporting here (PGS001855 reports partial-r only; PGS002066 shows a low AUROC=0.57 and an OR with CI crossing 1, plus unknown covariates). PGS004253 is the only disease-focused ulcerative-colitis-specific publication in the set (Nat Commun; PRS for UC predicting ICI-mediated colitis) while still using a direct UC endpoint (phenotyping_reported: Ulcerative colitis) and basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). It also reports strong effect-size evidence (OR=1.84 per SD with tight CI) and a full-model AUROC=0.66 in Europeans. Main limitation: the reported AUROC appears to be full-model (no explicit PGS-only AUROC/R2), and the study framing includes a therapy-related application context, so PRS-only comparability versus the framework models’ explicit PGS-only metrics remains uncertain—hence sub-optimal and not high-confidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000611",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported is bladder cancer and phenotyping_reported is bladder cancer / cancer of bladder / incident bladder cancer / urothelial carcinoma). No candidate reports PRS-only AUROC or PRS-only R2, so full-model AUROC values are not directly comparable as standalone PRS performance and several high AUROCs (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 ~0.80) are likely dominated by covariates (age/sex/smoking/BMI), consistent with the domain guidance.\n\nWithin the Cancer PRSweb family (PGS000607–PGS000616), the small-variant “GWAS Hits” models show materially stronger effect-size evidence than the genome-wide shrinkage models: PGS000611 reports OR per SD 1.284 (95% CI 1.211–1.361) and a strong top-1% vs rest OR 2.66 (1.79–3.93), with a similar full-model AUROC (~0.567) to its close siblings. Compared with the near-identical MGI-based GWAS-hits sibling PGS000610/PGS000612 (OR 1.292 but weaker top-1% OR 1.47 with wide CI), PGS000611 has stronger extreme-tail separation and is evaluated in a larger UKB-based validation (n=13,530 vs n=7,622).\n\nThis remains sub-optimal rather than high-quality because the pool lacks PRS-comparable (PGS-only) discrimination metrics, and multiple endpoint-faithful alternatives exist with broadly similar evidence (e.g., PGS000613 has very similar OR 1.301 and even higher top-1% OR 2.91, but without additional differentiating PRS-only metrics or clearer endpoint/covariate advantages, the best choice is not unequivocal)."
}
